// ── how connector numbers were attached to the two readings, and what discriminates the join ───

// NEITHER READING PRINTS A J-NUMBER. The firmware names (socket, controller, slot-in-channel);
// SMBIOS names "DIMM n". The connector field on both lists above is therefore a JOIN, and it is
// declared as one rather than presented as a direct reading.
//
// THE UPSTREAM SIDE IS ALREADY MODELLED AND IS NOT RE-TRANSCRIBED HERE.
// extdeps.ampere.mt_collins_product_brief.memory_population carries, from Tables 12-13 of Ampere's
// Getting Started Guide, that connectors pair as {J1,J2}, {J3,J4} … {J31,J32} with pair k on MCU k,
// that J1..J16 serve socket 0 and J17..J32 serve socket 1, and that the 16-DIMM 1DPC population
// occupies the odd member of every pair. This module consumes that and adds no second copy of it.
//
// THE JOIN IS: SMBIOS "DIMM n" is connector Jn on socket 0 and J(16+n) on socket 1, taken in the
// order the SMBIOS Type 17 structures appear.
//
// WHAT DISCRIMINATES IT, which matters because an ordering hypothesis that merely "looks right"
// establishes nothing. Three independent checks, and the first is the sharp one:
//
//   1. A UNIQUE PART LANDS ON A UNIQUE POSITION. HMA82GR7CJR8N-VK occurs exactly ONCE in the
//      machine. The firmware puts it at SK0 MC5; the SMBIOS table puts it at "DIMM 11". Under this
//      join those are the same connector, J11, and MCU5's odd member is J11. A wrong ordering
//      would have to place a one-of-sixteen part on the one position that agrees with the pair
//      table, which no shift or reversal of the sequence does.
//
//   2. THE RANK SEQUENCE REPRODUCES INDEPENDENTLY OF PART NUMBER. SMBIOS's own Rank field reads
//      1,2,1,1,2,2,1,2 across socket 0's populated slots and 1,1,2,1,1,1,1,2 across socket 1's;
//      the firmware's 1R/2R column reads the same two sequences in MCU order. That is a second
//      field, read by a different agent from a different table, agreeing position for position.
//
//   3. THE POPULATED SET IS EXACTLY THE ODD CONNECTORS, which is what the Getting Started Guide's
//      16-DIMM row independently specifies, and the firmware independently reports every module at
//      S0 — slot 0 of its channel — on all sixteen channels.
//
// WHAT IS NOT CLAIMED. This does not establish that the board silkscreen reads "J11" beside the
// eleventh socket-0 connector; no one has looked at the board. It establishes that the connector
// this repository calls J11, as Ampere's tables define it, is the one carrying the CJR8N module.
// A physical-label check is a photograph, not a software reading, and is out of scope here.
type MtCollins1ConnectorIdentificationBasis {
  smbios_to_connector_rule: NonEmptyStr
  discriminating_witness: NonEmptyStr
  corroborating_checks: NonEmptyStr
  not_established: NonEmptyStr
  upstream_pair_authority: DeclarationRef
}

data mtcollins1_connector_identification_basis: MtCollins1ConnectorIdentificationBasis = MtCollins1ConnectorIdentificationBasis {
  smbios_to_connector_rule: "SMBIOS Type 17 DeviceLocator \"DIMM n\" is connector Jn for socket 0 and J(16+n) for socket 1, in Type 17 structure order. SMBIOS prints no J-number; this is a join, not a reading.",
  discriminating_witness: "HMA82GR7CJR8N-VK occurs exactly once in the unit. Firmware places it at SK0 MC5; SMBIOS places it at \"DIMM 11\"; the Getting Started Guide's pair table places MCU5's 1DPC member at J11. A one-of-sixteen part agreeing with the pair table at the single position that carries it is not reproduced by any shift or reversal of the sequence.",
  corroborating_checks: "SMBIOS Rank reads 1,2,1,1,2,2,1,2 (socket 0) and 1,1,2,1,1,1,1,2 (socket 1) across populated slots in order, matching the firmware's 1R/2R column in MCU order field-for-field; and the populated set is exactly the odd connectors, which the guide's 16-DIMM row specifies and the firmware corroborates by reporting every module at slot S0 of its channel.",
  not_established: "That the physical board silkscreen reads J11 at that connector. No one has inspected the board; that is a photograph, not a software reading.",
  upstream_pair_authority: DeclarationRef {
    module_path: "extdeps.ampere.mt_collins_product_brief.memory_population",
    decl_name: "mt_collins_channel_connector_pairs",
    field: WholeDeclaration,
  },
}

// ── what the census does NOT answer, recorded so nobody re-runs the boot to look for it ────────

// THE CHANNEL MAP IS NOT OBTAINABLE FROM EDAC ON THIS PLATFORM and re-booting will not change
// that, because the cause is APEI firmware-first mode rather than a missing module or a boot
// option. Recording the negative WITH its mechanism is the difference between a fact and an
// unfinished errand: without it the next reader sees "no csrow tree" and schedules another boot.
data mtcollins1_edac_channel_map_unavailable_reason: NonEmptyStr = "ghes_edac is an error-reporting shim under ACPI APEI firmware-first mode (dmesg: \"GHES: APEI firmware first mode is enabled by APEI bit\"), not a memory-controller topology driver. It registers one synthetic controller and mirrors the SMBIOS Type 17 device list so a GHES error record has a DIMM to name. No csrow or channel node exists to read, on any boot, without a native Altra memory-controller EDAC driver that does not exist in mainline. The channel-resolved reading comes from the boot firmware's DRAM training output instead, which is carried by mtcollins1_firmware_channel_bindings and is strictly richer, naming slot-within-channel as well as controller."

// ── modelling obligations this census surfaces ─────────────────────────────────────────────────

// A PART THIS UNIT ACTUALLY CARRIES HAS NO CATALOG ROW. extdeps.memory.sk_hynix carries
// hma82gr7afr4n_vk_catalog and hma82gr7afr8n_vk_catalog; HMA82GR7CJR8N-VK appears nowhere in the
// corpus. Until it has a row, no HostMemoryPopulation can be authored for this unit, because
// MemoryPopulationRow takes a DramModuleCatalogRow and one of the three installed parts has none.
//
// THE ROW IS NOT AUTHORED HERE, and that restraint is the point. Its properties are known to this
// module only because the firmware and SMBIOS reported them on one machine; a catalog row is an
// upstream fact about a part SK Hynix sells, and minting it from our own boot log would be the
// provenance inversion DESIGN section 3 forbids — a report rendered as an upstream fact. The row
// belongs in extdeps.memory.sk_hynix, cited to SK Hynix's own module documentation, and this
// module's job is to say the obligation exists and what was observed.
type MtCollins1CensusModellingObligation {
  subject: NonEmptyStr
  why_it_blocks: NonEmptyStr
  observed_properties: NonEmptyStr
  correct_home: NonEmptyStr
}

data mtcollins1_uncatalogued_part_obligation: MtCollins1CensusModellingObligation = MtCollins1CensusModellingObligation {
  subject: "HMA82GR7CJR8N-VK, installed at connector J11 (socket 0, MCU5), serial 53BC6E37.",
  why_it_blocks: "extdeps.memory.sk_hynix has no catalog row for it, so no MemoryPopulationRow and therefore no HostMemoryPopulation can be authored for mtcollins1 the way gunbc.fleet.fleet_intent authors srv1..srv4.",
  observed_properties: "Reported by this unit as 16 GB, DDR4, registered, ECC, 2 ranks, x8 chip width, configured at 2666 MT/s (firmware DRAM training and SMBIOS Type 17 agree on rank, width and data rate).",
  correct_home: "extdeps.memory.sk_hynix, cited to SK Hynix module documentation. NOT authored from this census: a catalog row minted from our own boot log would render a local report as an upstream fact.",
}

// THE SAME-CHANNEL RANK RULE IS NOT IN THE CORPUS EITHER. The purchasing decision this census was
// commissioned to inform turns on OCP Mt. Jade's constraint that a single channel may not mix 1R
// and 2R modules, and nothing under extdeps.ocp.mt_jade carries it. Until it does, the per-connector
// rank requirement that follows from the bindings above is a conclusion a human is drawing from a
// document the corpus has not modelled, and it must not be authored here as though it were derived.
data mtcollins1_rank_mixing_rule_obligation: MtCollins1CensusModellingObligation = MtCollins1CensusModellingObligation {
  subject: "Same-channel rank mixing constraint for Mt. Jade / Mt. Collins (no 1R and 2R on one channel).",
  why_it_blocks: "The fill decision for the sixteen empty connectors is exactly this rule applied to mtcollins1_firmware_channel_bindings. Without the rule modelled, the required rank per empty connector cannot be DERIVED in the substrate and would have to be asserted.",
  observed_properties: "Not an observation of this unit at all; it is an upstream platform constraint. This unit merely makes it decision-relevant by carrying a mixed 1R/2R population across its sixteen channels.",
  correct_home: "extdeps.ocp.mt_jade, cited to the OCP Mt. Jade specification's memory population table.",
}

data mtcollins1_census_modelling_obligations: List<MtCollins1CensusModellingObligation> = [
  mtcollins1_uncatalogued_part_obligation,
  mtcollins1_rank_mixing_rule_obligation,
]

// ── why the second capture took five attempts, and the defect that caused it ───────────────────

// THE HOST WOULD NOT BOOT THE PRESENTED MEDIUM, AND THE REASON IS A FIELD THE BOOT-HANDOFF MODEL
// DOES NOT CARRY. IPMI boot parameter 5 encodes TWO decisions: the device selector, and the BIOS
// boot type (byte 1 bit 5 — 0 = PC-compatible/legacy, 1 = EFI). gunbc.machine_intake_oob_boot_handoff
// and gunbc.machine_intake_mtcollins1_actuate model the SELECTOR and have no concept of the boot
// type at all: `efiboot`, `boot_type` and `legacy` appear nowhere in that path. So every handoff
// this repository performs requests the device under the DEFAULT boot type, which reads back as
// "BIOS PC Compatible (legacy) boot".
//
// ON aarch64 THERE IS NO LEGACY BIOS BOOT PATH. A legacy-boot request for the virtual CD is
// therefore unsatisfiable, the firmware cannot honour it, and it falls through to its own boot
// order — PXE, which nothing on this segment answers, and then the UEFI shell. That is the
// observed behaviour on every failed attempt.
//
// SETTING THE BIT FIXES IT, MEASURED: `chassis bootdev cdrom options=efiboot,persistent` reads back
// "BIOS EFI boot" / "Force Boot from CD/DVD", and the very next reset booted the census image and
// produced the capture this module now carries. One argv word is the whole difference.
//
// THE MODEL SAID THE HANDOFF SUCCEEDED EVERY TIME, WHICH IS THE DEEPER DEFECT AND THE REASON THIS
// SURVIVED. oob_boot_handoff mints BootHandoffCompleted once the override reads back with the
// expected rendering and the power action is accepted. Both were true on every failed attempt: the
// selector really did say "Force Boot from CD/DVD", and the power cycle really was accepted. The
// arm confirms a PRECONDITION and reports it as an EFFECT, so a handoff that provably never booted
// anything is indistinguishable, in the model, from one that did. That is DESIGN section 5's
// specification-without-execution, and it is why a legacy/EFI mismatch could persist undetected
// across an entire intake lane.
//
// Fixing the argv without fixing the postcondition would remove this instance and leave the class:
// the next unsatisfiable boot request would again be reported as completed.
type MtCollins1BootDeliveryDefect {
  missing_field: NonEmptyStr
  why_it_fails_here: NonEmptyStr
  measured_fix: NonEmptyStr
  postcondition_defect: NonEmptyStr
}

data mtcollins1_boot_delivery_defect: MtCollins1BootDeliveryDefect = MtCollins1BootDeliveryDefect {
  missing_field: "IPMI boot parameter 5 BIOS boot type (byte 1 bit 5: 0 = PC-compatible/legacy, 1 = EFI). Absent from gunbc.machine_intake_oob_boot_handoff and gunbc.machine_intake_mtcollins1_actuate, which model the device selector alone.",
  why_it_fails_here: "The default reads back as \"BIOS PC Compatible (legacy) boot\". aarch64 has no legacy BIOS boot path, so a legacy-boot request for the virtual CD is unsatisfiable; the firmware falls through to PXE (unanswered on this segment) and then the UEFI shell.",
  measured_fix: "chassis bootdev cdrom options=efiboot,persistent reads back \"BIOS EFI boot\" and the next reset booted the census image, producing mtcollins1-host-capture.txt.",
  postcondition_defect: "oob_boot_handoff mints BootHandoffCompleted from override-readback plus power-action-accepted, never from host-side evidence that the intended device booted. Both preconditions held on every failed attempt, so the model reported success throughout. The argv fix without a real postcondition would close this instance and leave the class.",
}

// ── the unit binding, CLOSED by a later capture ────────────────────────────────────────────────

// THIS RECEIPT'S FIRST CAPTURE BOUND ONLY TO THE BOARD MODEL and said so. It carried the kernel's
// `DMI: FOXCONN Mt. Collins/Mt. Collins` line, which names a MODEL, and no SMBIOS Type 1/2/3, so
// the board serial appeared nowhere in its bytes; what tied it to unit 1 was the delivery path,
// which is provenance held by the author rather than a fact a reader can check inside the artifact.
//
// A SECOND CAPTURE CLOSES IT, and the closure is an EQUALITY BETWEEN TWO INDEPENDENT READS rather
// than a stronger assertion of the same one. The host, reading its own SMBIOS Type 2 under Linux,
// reports board serial 02030A800TEXFT02L. gunbc.machine_intake_mtcollins1_access_observation
// reports the same serial from the CONTROLLER's IPMI FRU, over a different transport, from a
// different agent, recorded days earlier. Host-side DMI and controller-side FRU agreeing on one
// serial is what makes this an observation OF A UNIT; either alone is an observation of a reading.
data mtcollins1_host_capture_artifact_path: NonEmptyStr = "artifacts/bmc/mtcollins1-host-capture.txt"

data mtcollins1_host_capture_byte_count: Int = 270880

data mtcollins1_host_capture_digest: ContentHash = Sha256Hash(Sha256Digest {
  hex: "a9b880c3ecd9ba611c1df9e7de1ab089839c5d8e92b743bf2e1bcd684100fd2a",
})

data mtcollins1_host_capture_observed_at: EpochMs = 1789428410000

// EVERY SECTION OF THAT CAPTURE CARRIES ITS ARGV AND ITS EXIT STATUS, which is what makes a
// missing reading distinguishable from a zero one. All nineteen sections exited 0, so no field
// below is standing on a command that failed quietly.
type MtCollins1SubjectBinding {
  smbios_board_serial: NonEmptyStr
  smbios_system_serial: NonEmptyStr
  smbios_system_uuid: NonEmptyStr
  baseboard_product: NonEmptyStr
  corroborating_controller_read: NonEmptyStr
}

data mtcollins1_subject_binding: MtCollins1SubjectBinding = MtCollins1SubjectBinding {
  smbios_board_serial: "02030A800TEXFT02L",
  smbios_system_serial: "MXX2080619",
  smbios_system_uuid: "d5b80e46-903a-1000-01e6-4cd68a3cc128",
  baseboard_product: "FOXCONN Mt. Collins, board version 02030A800-600-G",
  corroborating_controller_read: "gunbc.machine_intake_mtcollins1_access_observation reads board serial 02030A800TEXFT02L from the controller's IPMI FRU. The host reads the same serial from its own SMBIOS Type 2 under Linux. Two transports, two agents, one serial.",
}

// THE FAILED ATTEMPT IS PRESERVED, WHICH IS THE POINT OF PRESERVING IT. Attempt 3 was built to
// close the binding gap above and to add a named memory workload with before/after EDAC counters.
// It never produced a host capture: the kernel loaded from the virtual CD and then panicked with
// "VFS: Unable to mount root fs on unknown-block(0,0)" — the MegaRAC remote-media session dropped
// out from under casper between loading the kernel and mounting the squashfs root. The controller
// reported redirection_status 100 immediately before the reset and status 1 afterwards.
//
// ITS BYTES ARE COMMITTED ANYWAY. The recurring failure mode this whole census exists to answer is
// that a decisive observation gets produced by ad-hoc probing and then lost because the probe's
// output was never a receipt; a FAILED probe is exactly as easy to lose and is evidence about the
// delivery route's reliability, which is a fact the boot-delivery model needs. Attempt 3 also
// produced a srv2-side observation before the boot — an ARP reachability reading with a typed
// verdict against the expected MAC, and the boot artifact's digest — and that file stands on its
// own regardless of the host never booting.
//
// WHY THERE IS NO ATTEMPT 5. Re-establishing the media after attempt 3 degraded further: stop-media
// and start-media both answered HTTP 500 and the session never returned to 100. This controller is
// the ONLY out-of-band path to the M1 canary, and hammering a degraded remote-media subsystem to
// buy a subject binding and a workload is not a trade worth making against the risk of losing that
// path. The run was stopped, the boot override cleared to "No override", and the controller left
// verified healthy over IPMI (ARP REACHABLE at the expected MAC, mc info firmware 0.32).
type MtCollins1CaptureAttemptFailure {
  attempt: Nat
  intent: NonEmptyStr
  observed_failure: NonEmptyStr
  preserved_artifacts: NonEmptyStr
  why_not_retried: NonEmptyStr
}

data mtcollins1_attempt3_failure: MtCollins1CaptureAttemptFailure = MtCollins1CaptureAttemptFailure {
  attempt: 3,
  intent: "Close the subject-binding gap (dmidecode type 1/2/3), record per-section argv and exit status, and add a named memory workload with EDAC counters read before and after, so the health reading would be a load-based one rather than a snapshot.",
  observed_failure: "The host loaded the kernel from the presented virtual CD and panicked: \"VFS: Unable to mount root fs on unknown-block(0,0)\". The remote-media session dropped between kernel load and root mount; redirection_status read 100 immediately before the reset and 1 after. No host capture was produced.",
  preserved_artifacts: "artifacts/bmc/mtcollins1-attempt3-sol.log (the console, carrying the panic) and artifacts/bmc/mtcollins1-attempt3-srv2-side.txt (the pre-boot ARP observation with a typed verdict against the expected MAC, plus the boot artifact digest). A failed attempt whose bytes are discarded reproduces the exact failure mode this census exists to end.",
  why_not_retried: "A further attempt refused before booting: stop-media and start-media both answered HTTP 500 and the session never returned to redirection_status 100. This controller is the only out-of-band path to the M1 canary, so driving a degraded remote-media subsystem harder was declined in favour of leaving the path intact. Boot override was cleared and the controller verified healthy over IPMI.",
}
